msgid "hardware.briefing.move_note"
msgstr "Mission folds into the lower-left corner. Reopen any page from the goal bar or Mission button. You can still move, fold and expand the window."

msgid "hardware.briefing.tutorial.1"
msgstr "Make the lamp show the opposite of input A.\n\nInput: A is one bit (1-bit), either 0 or 1.\nOutput: LAMP; it should be 1 when A=0, and 0 when A=1.\nParts: NOT gates. Place, move and connect them freely.\n\nFinish five actions: wire, change the input, run, erase a wire, reconnect. Then continue to the next challenge."

msgid "hardware.briefing.tutorial.gates"
msgstr "This challenge uses a [[not_gate|NOT gate]]: 0 in gives 1 out; 1 in gives 0 out.\n\nSelect a component to inspect its ports and role. Underlined blue terms open the Handbook; later concepts become available as you progress."

msgid "hardware.briefing.tutorial.2"
msgstr "The toolbox is open: drag a card onto the canvas, or click it to place repeatedly. Esc or right-click ends placement.\n\nDrag from an output to an input of the same width to [[wire|connect]] them. Outputs send; inputs receive. Drag a part body to move it."

msgid "hardware.briefing.tutorial.3"
msgstr "Toggle A in the [[test_bench|Test Bench]] and choose Run Practice Circuit. Check both 0 and 1. After running, erase a wire and reconnect it; Mission tracks these actions.\n\nAfter Start, Mission becomes a short goal. Expand it to revisit every page; the Mission taskbar button always brings it back. Request Hint only when you want solution guidance."

msgid "hardware.briefing.half_adder.1"
msgstr "Teach a circuit to add: build a [[half_adder|Half Adder]] for A+B.\n\nInputs: A and B, one bit each.\nOutputs: [[sum|SUM]] is the low bit; [[carry|CARRY]] carries into the next bit.\nExample: 1+1 is binary 10, so SUM=0 and CARRY=1.\n\nUse AND, OR and NOT in any valid circuit. Pass all four test rows, then seal your HalfAdder for use in the next challenge."

msgid "hardware.briefing.half_adder.2"
msgstr "A [[truth_table|truth table]] lists every possible input and its required output. Two inputs with two states give four cases. Build a circuit that produces the SUM and CARRY shown in every row below."

msgid "hardware.briefing.half_adder.3"
msgstr "Available tools: [[and_gate|AND]], [[or_gate|OR]] and [[not_gate|NOT]]. All ports are one bit wide.\n\nAND: output 1 when both inputs are 1.\nOR: output 1 when at least one input is 1.\nNOT: output the opposite of its input.\n\nDrag new components from Components and combine them your way. The full test checks the specified outputs and accepts different legal circuits."

msgid "hardware.briefing.half_adder.4"
msgstr "Use the [[test_bench|Test Bench]] to switch A/B one case at a time, and use a [[debug_run|debug run]] to find mistakes. Once all four cases work, run the [[official_test|Complete Truth Table test]]. Passing every row enables [[encapsulation|sealing]], which turns the verified circuit into a reusable HalfAdder component."

msgid "hardware.briefing.full_adder.1"
msgstr "Extend addition to another bit. CIN is the carry arriving from the previous bit. A [[full_adder|Full Adder]] includes it in the sum.\n\nInputs: A, B and CIN, one bit each.\nOutputs: SUM is the low bit of A+B+CIN; COUT is the high bit.\nExample: 1+1+1 is binary 11, so both outputs are 1.\n\nReuse your HalfAdder and choose your own wiring. The full test checks all eight input combinations."

msgid "hardware.briefing.full_adder.2"
msgstr "A, B, and CIN are 1-bit. SUM is the low bit of A+B+CIN; COUT is the high bit.\n\nThe inventory includes two sealed [[half_adder|Half Adders]], OR, and other parts in Components. A Half Adder has two inputs and SUM/CARRY outputs. Build freely using these interfaces; full tests check all eight input cases."

msgid "hardware.briefing.full_adder.3"
msgstr "The key acceptance case is A=B=CIN=1: SUM=1 and COUT=1. Pass all eight inputs, then seal the [[full_adder|Full Adder]]."

msgid "hardware.briefing.alu.1"
msgstr "Let the same inputs perform different operations. An [[alu|ALU]] selects an arithmetic or logic operation using control bits.\n\nInputs: A, B, CIN, OP1 and OP0, all one bit.\nRules: 00 AND; 01 OR; 10 addition; 11 NOT A.\nOutputs: RESULT and the addition carry CARRY.\n\nBuild freely with your existing parts. All four operations must pass the full test. Your one-bit ALU can then be reused in a four-bit ALU4."

msgid "hardware.briefing.alu.2"
msgstr "Operation specification: OP1 OP0 = 00 → A AND B; 01 → A OR B; 10 → A+B+CIN; 11 → NOT A. RESULT is the low bit. CARRY is checked only for ADD; other operations do not check CARRY.\n\nA [[multiplexer|MUX]] selects a data input using the binary value at its selector ports; it does not perform arithmetic. Inspect components for every port and width."

msgid "hardware.briefing.alu.3"
msgstr "Debug each OP choice, then run all official tests. Passing creates ALU4 by placing four copies of your verified one-bit structure side by side."

msgid "hardware.briefing.latch.1"
msgstr "Make a circuit remember. Ordinary gates read the current inputs; an [[sr_latch|SR Latch]] also retains a state.\n\nInputs: S is the Set control and R the Reset control, both one bit.\nOutputs: Q is the stored bit; NQ is its opposite.\nRules: S=1, R=0 sets Q=1; S=0, R=1 sets Q=0; both 0 holds. S=R=1 is outside this level’s test cases.\n\nBuild freely with NOR gates. The full sequence checks that the state survives after the input returns to 0."

msgid "hardware.briefing.latch.2"
msgstr "S and R mean [[set_reset|Set / Reset]]: S=1 sets Q to 1, R=1 clears Q to 0, and S=R=0 must keep the last Q. S=R=1 is a conflicting input that this level does not use."

msgid "hardware.briefing.latch.3"
msgstr "Specifications: a [[nor_gate|NOR gate]] outputs 1 only when both inputs are 0. All ports are 1-bit.\n\nExample: reset with R=1 → hold 0 at S=R=0 → set with S=1 → hold 1 at S=R=0. Legal latch feedback follows this level’s state rules. Full tests check reset, hold, set, and holding again."

msgid "hardware.briefing.latch.4"
msgstr "Test in order: S=1,R=0 writes 1; S=0,R=0 holds it; S=0,R=1 clears it; then hold again. Pass every official case and seal SRLatch."

msgid "hardware.briefing.register.1"
msgstr "Give storage a “save now” switch. A [[register|register]] receives data at D and uses [[d_q|LOAD]] to decide when to save it.\n\nInputs: D and LOAD, one bit each. Output: the stored bit Q.\nRule: LOAD=1 saves D; LOAD=0 keeps Q unchanged.\nExample: save 1, turn LOAD off, then change D to 0. Q must remain 1.\n\nReuse your SRLatch and the allowed gates. Pass the full test to seal Register1 and obtain the four-bit Register4."

msgid "hardware.briefing.register.2"
msgstr "D, LOAD, and Q are 1-bit. LOAD=1 stores D; LOAD=0 holds Q.\n\nExample: D=1 and LOAD=1 gives Q=1. Then LOAD=0 and D=0 must leave Q=1.\n\nAvailable parts include NOT, AND, and your sealed SR Latch. Its S sets, R resets, and S=R=0 holds; Inspector keeps these port rules available."

msgid "hardware.briefing.register.3"
msgstr "After writing, return LOAD to 0 and change D; Q must not follow it. Pass all official cases, seal Register1, and the game will create four-bit Register4."

msgid "hardware.briefing.ram.1"
msgstr "Give two storage locations their own data. An address chooses which location to read or write: this is [[ram|RAM]].\n\nADDR[1] selects address 0 or 1. DATA[4] is the value to write. WRITE[1] enables writing. OUT[4] shows the selected location.\n\nFour bits represent binary 0000 to 1111, or decimal 0 to 15.\n\nReuse Register4, Decoder and Word MUX in your own circuit. The full test checks independent writes, holding and readback: one location must not overwrite the other."

msgid "hardware.briefing.ram.2"
msgstr "Interface specification: ADDR[1] selects address 0 or 1. DATA[4] is the value to write; WRITE[1]=1 updates only the selected address. OUT[4] shows the currently addressed word.\n\nDecoder activates a selected output from ADDR and enable. Register[4] stores its input when LOAD=1. Word MUX selects an input word. Inspector keeps these port rules available."

msgid "hardware.briefing.ram.3"
msgstr "Write different values to addresses 0 and 1, turn writing off, then switch addresses to confirm the values stay separate. Pass the official tests and seal RAM2x4."

msgid "hardware.briefing.cpu.1"
msgstr "Join parts that calculate and remember into a computer that follows instructions.\n\nInputs: OPCODE[2] selects an action; ARG[4] is a number supplied by the instruction; ADDR[1] selects memory.\nOutputs: ACC[4] is the number in the accumulator; MEM[4] is the selected memory value. Four-bit values range from 0 to 15; overflow keeps the low four bits.\n\nImplement the four actions in the instruction table. Use milestones to inspect parts of your circuit in any order. Pass the complete seven-step program to seal TinyComputer."

msgid "hardware.briefing.cpu.2"
msgstr "The Test Bench input OP is the 2-bit operation code (OPCODE). The large number and the two cells below show the same code:\n\n0 = 00  LOAD_IMM  — copy ARG[4] into ACC\n1 = 01  ADD_IMM   — add ARG[4] to ACC\n2 = 10  LOAD_MEM  — copy RAM[ADDR] into ACC\n3 = 11  STORE_MEM — copy ACC into RAM[ADDR]\n\nIMM is the [[immediate|immediate value]] written in the instruction."

msgid "hardware.briefing.cpu.3"
msgstr "Module specifications: ALU performs 4-bit arithmetic. ACC stores its input when LOAD=1 and holds when LOAD=0. RAM reads the row at ADDR and writes when WRITE=1. A MUX selects one of two 4-bit inputs using SEL.\n\nController reads 2-bit OPCODE and produces four 1-bit controls: SOURCE_SEL, RESULT_SEL, ACC_LOAD, MEM_WRITE. Click a module body for its port and selection rules; these are task specifications."

msgid "hardware.briefing.cpu.4"
msgstr "Stage goals:\nA Operand interfaces: opcode, immediate value, arithmetic input.\nB Compute and store: addition, loading, holding.\nC Memory read: address and addressed word.\nD Memory write: target row and write enable.\n\nCheck Interfaces only checks whether the relevant inputs are connected. It does not certify correctness or add wires. The full test checks ACC and RAM state after every instruction."

msgid "hardware.briefing.cpu.5"
msgstr "The full test executes seven [[program|instructions]] in order. Each step shows the instruction, prior state, expected result and actual result.\n\nIf a step differs, check what its opcode should do, then inspect the relevant ports. The specification and opcode table remain available.\n\nPass every step to seal TinyComputer. Next, use your own computer to observe data moving during LOAD and STORE."

msgid "hardware.briefing.load_store.1"
msgstr "No new wiring is needed. Run your sealed computer and watch two actions: [[load|LOAD]] brings data from [[ram|RAM]] to the [[cpu|CPU]], while [[store|STORE]] sends the CPU result back.\n\nRAM → LOAD → CPU → STORE → RAM"

msgid "hardware.briefing.load_store.2"
msgstr "Run your sealed computer with the fixed [[program|program]] and watch ACC and RAM. When the final ADD changes ACC, does the value at address 0 change too? Follow the [[trace|Trace]] to distinguish calculation from STORE. In the next chapter, investigate the time spent moving data."

msgid "hardware.briefing.generic.goal"
msgstr "Identify the fixed inputs, fixed outputs, and allowed components, then split the task into smaller signal relationships. Test each section as you build instead of copying a complete circuit first."

msgid "hardware.briefing.generic.verify"
msgstr "Use a single debug input to confirm that signals follow the intended route, then run every official case. Only a complete official pass can seal the current topology as a player-owned component."

