msgstr "Connect all six request, write, and read routes, apply the program, and pass both official cases."

msgid "system.level.assembly.briefing.1"
msgstr "You built a four-bit computer. Now use a separate eight-bit teaching model to investigate time spent computing and moving data.\n\n[[cpu|CPU]] computes, [[ram|RAM]] stores data, and the [[bus|Bus]] transports it.\nCPU → Bus → RAM\nCPU ← Bus ← RAM"

msgid "system.level.assembly.briefing.2"
msgstr "Connect the request, write-data and read-data routes: six [[wire|wire]] segments in total. The supplied program is already applied: read a value, add 1, then write it back.\n\nIn the [[test_bench|Test Bench]], select Run all official cases and check both results. Use [[apply|Confirm and apply]] only after editing the program draft."

msgid "system.level.cpu_speed.title"
msgstr "AFTER THE CPU GETS FASTER"

msgid "system.level.cpu_speed.description"
msgstr "This memory-heavy sum mixes arithmetic with repeated data trips. Predict whether 4× faster arithmetic makes the whole run 4× faster, then test it."

msgid "system.level.cpu_speed.objective"
msgstr "Lock a prediction, run Eco as the baseline, then change only CPU to Fast and explain total-cycle change from compute versus WAIT."

msgid "system.level.cpu_speed.briefing.1"
msgstr "The CPU adds numbers but also waits for RAM. A faster CPU does not make RAM or the Bus faster. First record a [[prediction|prediction]]: will the whole run improve a lot, a little, or hardly at all? It is only your starting idea."

msgid "system.level.cpu_speed.briefing.2"
msgstr "Run Eco CPU first as a [[baseline|baseline]], the starting point for comparison. Then change only to Fast CPU and keep RAM, Bus, program, and cases fixed. Changing one cause at a time is a [[controlled_change|controlled comparison]]."

msgid "system.level.cpu_speed.briefing.3"
msgstr "Compare total [[cycle|cycles]], then open the [[profiler|Profiler]] and compare compute time with [[cpu_wait|CPU WAIT]]. Pass the same official cases with both CPUs to finish."

msgid "system.level.ram_wait.title"
msgstr "WHO IS THE CPU WAITING FOR?"

msgid "system.level.ram_wait.description"
msgstr "Predict where CPU time goes, then follow repeated requests, RAM service, returned data, and the CPU's visible WAIT state."

msgid "system.level.ram_wait.objective"
msgstr "Lock a prediction, keep CPU, Bus, program, and tests fixed, then change only RAM and explain the CPU-wait delta."

msgid "system.level.ram_wait.briefing.1"
msgstr "A faster [[cpu|CPU]] shortened computation, but [[cpu_wait|waiting]] stayed the same. The CPU still cannot continue until its data returns.\n\nKeep the fast CPU and follow the request, [[ram|RAM]] service and return path. Predict whether computation or waiting dominates, or whether they are similar."

msgid "system.level.ram_wait.briefing.2"
msgstr "Keep Fast CPU and Bus8 fixed. Run Slow RAM, then change only to Fast RAM. The [[trace|Trace]] shows request → RAM service → returned data."

msgid "system.level.ram_wait.briefing.3"
msgstr "Use the same program and official cases both times. Compare CPU WAIT in the Profiler; because only RAM changed, that difference tells you where the waiting came from."

msgid "system.level.bus_width.title"
msgstr "HOW MANY TRIPS FOR ONE BYTE?"

msgid "system.level.bus_width.description"
msgstr "The same byte crosses each Bus width; predict how serialization changes, then count the stages on the exact wire."

msgid "system.level.bus_width.objective"
msgstr "Lock a prediction, run Bus2 as the baseline, then change only Bus width and inspect segmented packets on the exact wires."

msgid "system.level.bus_width.briefing.1"
msgstr "[[bandwidth|Bandwidth]] is the number of bits a [[bus|Bus]] can carry per cycle. Each value here has 8 bits. Bus2 carries 2 bits per cycle; Bus8 carries 8.\n\nWhen a value does not fit in one trip, it must be split across trips: [[serialization|serialization]]. Predict the trips each channel needs, then observe the transfers."

msgid "system.level.bus_width.briefing.2"
msgstr "Lock a prediction and run Bus2. Keep CPU, RAM, program, and data fixed, then change only to Bus8. Watch how many packet stages cross the wire and pass both official runs."

msgid "system.level.bottleneck.title"
msgstr "BOTTLENECK INVESTIGATION"

msgid "system.level.bottleneck.description"
msgstr "Investigate one exact system across 4, 16, and 64 items. Use raw totals, CPU WAIT, workload growth, and Trace flow before the full breakdown is revealed."

msgid "system.level.bottleneck.objective"
msgstr "Pass the 4/16/64 official cases, submit CPU, RAM, Bus, or mixed as your first diagnosis, then compare it with the revealed breakdown."

msgid "system.level.bottleneck.briefing.1"
msgstr "A system is often limited by its busiest or slowest part. That main limit is the [[bottleneck|bottleneck]]. This level grows one [[workload|workload]] from 4 values to 16 and 64."

msgid "system.level.bottleneck.briefing.2"
msgstr "Run the 4, 16, and 64 official cases. Before seeing the full answer, compare total cycles, CPU WAIT, and data trips. Ask which kind of time grows fastest as the work grows."

msgid "system.level.bottleneck.briefing.3"
msgstr "Choose CPU, RAM, Bus, or mixed as the main limit. Only after that choice does the full breakdown open. Match your diagnosis to the [[profiler|Profiler]] evidence to finish the chapter."

