msgid "chapter2.level.unknown.title"
msgstr "Unknown Investigation"

msgid "chapter2.level.unknown.description"
msgstr "Invalid Chapter 2 content."

msgid "chapter2.level.distant_reads.title"
msgstr "Why Go All the Way Back?"

msgid "chapter2.level.distant_reads.description"
msgstr "Observe one correct scan where every requested value crosses the Bus to RAM."

msgid "chapter2.level.distant_reads.objective"
msgstr "Run once. Follow several loads from CPU to RAM and back, then identify what the CPU spends most of its time doing."

msgid "chapter2.level.distant_reads.briefing.1"
msgstr "The CPU adds quickly, but it needs data first. Every [[load|load]] takes the same long route:\n\n[[cpu|CPU]] → [[bus|Bus]] → [[ram|RAM]] → Bus → CPU\n\nThe CPU must stop until data returns; that time is [[cpu_wait|CPU WAIT]]."

msgid "chapter2.level.distant_reads.briefing.2"
msgstr "Run one official case and follow several trips with Next evidence. The [[trace|Trace]] shows where every step went. Then choose the explanation that best matches the evidence; no program change is needed."

msgid "chapter2.level.distant_reads.learned"
msgstr "Every load made the same distant round trip. Arithmetic stayed short while CPU WAIT accumulated along the data path."

msgid "chapter2.level.distant_reads.judgment.repeated_ram"
msgstr "The CPU waits because every value travels to RAM and back."

msgid "chapter2.level.distant_reads.judgment.cpu_math"
msgstr "The additions themselves consume most cycles."

msgid "chapter2.level.distant_reads.judgment.result_store"
msgstr "Writing the final result is the repeated delay."

msgid "chapter2.level.nearby_storage.title"
msgstr "Leave It Nearby"

msgid "chapter2.level.nearby_storage.description"
msgstr "Add one tiny fast store and compare the same program and data before and after."

msgid "chapter2.level.nearby_storage.objective"
msgstr "Capture a direct-RAM baseline, enable nearby storage, and rerun. Find the first far fetch and later nearby returns in Trace."

msgid "chapter2.level.nearby_storage.briefing.1"
msgstr "The last level went to RAM for every load. Add a tiny, fast store nearby: the first request still travels far, but later requests for the same data can return nearby.\n\nCPU → nearby store → Bus → RAM"

msgid "chapter2.level.nearby_storage.briefing.2"
msgstr "The direct-memory result is kept as a [[baseline|baseline]]. Enable the one-line nearby store and rerun the same program and data. Find the first far fetch and later nearby returns; this is a [[before_after|Before → After comparison]]."

msgid "chapter2.level.nearby_storage.learned"
msgstr "The nearby hardware is a Cache. A Miss fetches a line from RAM; later requests to that line are Hits and avoid the distant trip."

msgid "chapter2.level.cache_failure.title"
msgstr "Why Didn't It Help?"

msgid "chapter2.level.cache_failure.description"
msgstr "Observe a Cache that is present but repeatedly replaces the line needed next."

msgid "chapter2.level.cache_failure.objective"
msgstr "Do not change the machine. Run the fixed column-first program and use address order plus Miss/Evict evidence to explain 321 cycles."

msgid "chapter2.level.cache_failure.briefing.1"
msgstr "The nearby store now has its proper name: [[cache|Cache]]. One small block it holds is a [[cache_line|Cache line]]. If needed data is absent, that is a [[miss|Miss]] and RAM must supply it. Replacing old data when space runs out is an [[evict|Evict]]."

msgid "chapter2.level.cache_failure.briefing.2"
msgstr "Keep the machine and [[program|program]] unchanged and run once. Follow several consecutive addresses in the [[trace|Trace]]: which [[cache_line|line]] returns each time? Is it still nearby when needed again? Use the [[miss|Miss]] and [[evict|Evict]] records to choose the explanation supported by the evidence."

msgid "chapter2.level.cache_failure.learned"
msgstr "Installing a Cache is not enough. The strided order cycles through four lines while only one fits, so useful data is displaced before reuse."

msgid "chapter2.level.cache_failure.judgment.replacement"
msgstr "The access order replaces each useful line before it is reused."

msgid "chapter2.level.cache_failure.judgment.slow_cpu"
msgstr "The CPU arithmetic became slower."

msgid "chapter2.level.cache_failure.judgment.wrong_result"
msgstr "The long run is caused by an incorrect result."

msgid "chapter2.level.access_order.title"
msgstr "Change the Order"

msgid "chapter2.level.access_order.description"
msgstr "Repair the previous run without replacing its one-line Cache."

msgid "chapter2.level.access_order.objective"
msgstr "Change and Apply only the loop order. Reach 105 cycles or less with the same data and one-line Cache."

msgid "chapter2.level.access_order.briefing.1"
msgstr "If Cache data is reused soon, the CPU avoids another distant trip. Keeping accesses close in address and time is called [[locality|locality]]. The last program jumped by columns; now try reading each row continuously."

msgid "chapter2.level.access_order.briefing.2"
msgstr "Open [[program|Program]] and change the nesting order of the two loops, or load a draft with a strategy button. Review it, then select [[apply|Apply program]].\n\nKeep hardware, data and [[cache|Cache]] size unchanged. Produce the correct official result in at most 105 [[cycle|cycles]]."

msgid "chapter2.level.access_order.learned"
msgstr "Contiguous access reused each fetched line before moving on. This exploitable closeness in time or address is Locality."

msgid "chapter2.level.working_set.title"
msgstr "The Order Is Already Right"

msgid "chapter2.level.working_set.description"
msgstr "Observe two well-ordered passes whose needed data still cannot remain nearby."

msgid "chapter2.level.working_set.objective"
msgstr "Run the fixed row-first workload. Compare pass 1 with pass 2 and explain why all four lines are fetched again."

msgid "chapter2.level.working_set.briefing.1"
msgstr "The program handles the same data twice; each round is a [[pass|pass]]. All data needed repeatedly during that phase is the [[working_set|working set]]. If it is larger than Cache, pass 1's data may be gone before pass 2."

msgid "chapter2.level.working_set.briefing.2"
msgstr "Run the fixed row-first task. Compare passes 1 and 2: which [[cache_line|lines]] are fetched from far away again? Compare the data that needs to stay nearby with the available capacity, then choose the explanation supported by the evidence."

msgid "chapter2.level.working_set.learned"
msgstr "The access order was good, but the phase needed four lines while only one fit. The data needed now is the Working Set."

msgid "chapter2.level.working_set.judgment.does_not_fit"
msgstr "The four-line working data does not fit in one nearby line between passes."

msgid "chapter2.level.working_set.judgment.bad_order"
msgstr "The program returned to column-first access."

msgid "chapter2.level.working_set.judgment.more_math"
msgstr "The second pass performs unusually expensive arithmetic."

msgid "chapter2.level.blocking.title"
msgstr "Do Less at Once"

msgid "chapter2.level.blocking.description"
msgstr "Keep the same one-line Cache and reorganize when the two passes work on each line."

msgid "chapter2.level.blocking.objective"
msgstr "Use Work Group to finish both passes for a small group before moving on. Reach 145 cycles or less without upgrading hardware."

msgid "chapter2.level.blocking.briefing.1"
msgstr "Instead of finishing pass 1 for all data and then pass 2, take a small [[work_group|work group]] and finish both passes before moving. This reordering is [[blocking|blocking]].\n\nOld: all pass 1 → all pass 2\nNew: group 1 both passes → group 2 both passes → …"

msgid "chapter2.level.blocking.briefing.2"
msgstr "Keep one-line Cache and the row-first program. Choose a nonzero Work Group, then watch whether both passes reuse each line before moving. Reach 145 cycles or less."

msgid "chapter2.level.blocking.learned"
msgstr "Keeping one active line nearby across both passes shrank the working set. This reorganization is Blocking, also called Tiling."

msgid "chapter2.level.capstone.title"
msgstr "Now Investigate"

msgid "chapter2.level.capstone.description"
msgstr "A new two-pass workload exposes all learned tools but gives no diagnosis hint."

msgid "chapter2.level.capstone.objective"
msgstr "Run and diagnose the original setup first. Change one factor for the first experiment; then you may combine strategies. The official result must be correct and take at most 145 cycles."

msgid "chapter2.level.capstone.briefing.1"
msgstr "No diagnosis is supplied. Run the given setup first and inspect total cycles, CPU WAIT, and data trips. The main cause limiting the whole run is its [[bottleneck|bottleneck]]. Decide where the evidence points."

msgid "chapter2.level.capstone.briefing.2"
msgstr "For the first change, alter only one thing: access order, Cache size, or Work Group. Use the [[trace|Trace]] for events and the [[profiler|Profiler]] for time, then make a Before → After comparison."

msgid "chapter2.level.capstone.briefing.3"
msgstr "After your first single-factor experiment, you may combine access order, cache size and work grouping. Finish with the original baseline recorded, an evidence-supported judgment, and a correct official result within 145 cycles.\n\nAfter reaching the target, keep comparing cycles and cost to improve your personal best."

msgid "chapter2.level.capstone.learned"
msgstr "You completed the full loop: observe, diagnose, change one cause, and compare. Multiple hardware/software solutions remain valid."

msgid "chapter2.level.capstone.judgment.repeated_far_fetch"
msgstr "The CPU repeatedly waits while data is fetched again from far memory."

msgid "chapter2.level.capstone.judgment.more_cpu_math"
msgstr "Most cycles are spent on CPU arithmetic."

msgid "chapter2.level.capstone.judgment.wrong_result"
msgstr "The machine is slow because the program computes the wrong result."

