msgid "overlap.handbook.arrival"
msgstr "Request ≠ arrival\nfetch / prefetch submits a request. The transfer engine finishes later. Compute uses ready data. The two engines may work independently; one engine cannot do two jobs at once."

msgid "overlap.handbook.wires"
msgstr "Width and connections\nCyan double cable = 8-bit data; gold thin wire = 1-bit state. Connect buffer ready to compute, and free to transfer. Port labels also show width.\n\nWires have no simulation delay. Multiple buffers on a device port are addressed by component name, not electrically shorted. This chapter uses an explicit simplified batch-transfer model."

msgid "overlap.handbook.overlap"
msgstr "Read the timeline\nHorizontal position is simulated time. Blue is transfer and purple is compute. Simultaneous blocks overlap; elapsed time is not the sum of both busy times.\n\nExample: 4 batches, transfer 6, compute 4. Serial takes 40; the schedule below takes 28 with 12 overlapping cycles. The same simulator generates these numbers."

msgid "overlap.handbook.buffers"
msgstr "Buffer lifecycle\nEmpty → filling → ready → in use → empty\nfetch requires empty storage; ready waits for arrival; consume begins use; compute completion frees storage. free waits for that signal.\n\nA fixed tick knows time has passed, not whether data is finished. Every batch needs the same safe handoff."

msgid "overlap.handbook.cache"
msgstr "Prefetch and Cache\nEach line holds one batch tagged by batch number. Fills and reads update LRU recency; a full cache evicts the least recently used line. Once read starts compute, its value is latched; later eviction does not alter that computation.\n\nPrefetch and read misses share one transfer engine and a two-entry FIFO, including the active transfer. A full queue stalls the issuer while active work continues. Cached or pending prefetches are coalesced."

msgid "overlap.handbook.limits"
msgstr "Timing depends on resources\nToo late: compute waits for data. Too early: useful data may be evicted and fetched again. Extra buffers do not increase the single transfer engine bandwidth.\n\nCompare public workloads using elapsed time, overlap, waits and actual requests. There is no arbitrary prefetch penalty."

msgid "overlap.handbook.commands"
msgstr "Instruction contract\nOne command per line; # starts a comment. Component names are case-insensitive. Issuing costs zero cycles; device work and waits advance time.\nfetch A 0: request batch 0 into A.\nready A / free A: wait for ready / empty.\nconsume A: asynchronously compute the batch in A.\nidle: wait for active compute.\ntick N: wait N cycles.\nwork N: synchronous independent work (first level only).\nprefetch 0: request batch 0 into Cache early.\nread 0: wait for cached batch 0, then start compute.\n\nProgram end drains already-started work. It does not request missing batches or repair invalid operations."

