msgid "terminology.term.bit_width.title"
msgstr "Width: how many bits?"

msgid "terminology.term.bit_width.body"
msgstr "One bit is 0 or 1, shown by a thin wire and round port. A multi-bit signal packs several bits into one bus, shown by a double-rail cable and square port.\n\nWidth sets the range, not the current value: four bits hold 0–15. A value of 1 on that bus still has four-bit width. Directly connected ports must have matching widths."

msgid "terminology.term.async_transfer.title"
msgstr "Request versus arrival"

msgid "terminology.term.async_transfer.body"
msgstr "fetch and prefetch issue transfers; returning from the instruction does not mean data has arrived. Use it after the completion event.\n\nTransfer and compute engines can each work on one task at once. Independent computation may overlap the wait. Wires add no cycles."

msgid "terminology.term.double_buffer.title"
msgstr "Two buffers and their states"

msgid "terminology.term.double_buffer.body"
msgstr "One buffer holds the data being used while another receives the next batch. Alternating them can reduce compute waiting.\n\nEach goes through empty, filling, ready and in use. ready permits consuming; free permits refilling. free becomes 1 only after computation finishes."

msgid "terminology.term.backpressure.title"
msgstr "Backpressure: wait when busy"

msgid "terminology.term.backpressure.body"
msgstr "Never overwrite data that the consumer still needs. Pause writes while free is 0 and resume when the buffer is empty: that is backpressure.\n\nready A waits for data; free A waits for an empty buffer; idle waits for computation. These wait for actual state instead of assuming a fixed number of cycles."

msgid "terminology.term.prefetch.title"
msgstr "Prefetch: request before use"

msgid "terminology.term.prefetch.body"
msgstr "prefetch requests future data while current work can continue. If that data has not arrived when read needs it, the read still waits.\n\nPrefetch and demand reads share the transfer engine. Cache fills and replaces automatically. Judge elapsed time, not hit count alone."

msgid "terminology.term.prefetch_distance.title"
msgstr "Look-ahead and eviction"

msgid "terminology.term.prefetch_distance.body"
msgstr "Requesting too late leaves compute waiting. Requesting too early may displace data still needed from a small cache.\n\nThe A/B example shows one wasted transfer: B replaces unused A, then A must be fetched again. Inspect eviction and repeated transfers before adjusting look-ahead."

msgid "terminology.term.request_queue.title"
msgstr "Request queue"

msgid "terminology.term.request_queue.body"
msgstr "The transfer engine handles one request at a time. A second may queue. Capacity is two including the active request.\n\nWhen full, issuing another request waits for a slot. More requests do not create more bandwidth or simultaneous transfers."

