msgid "exploration.selector4.title"
msgstr "One of four"

msgid "exploration.selector4.description"
msgstr "Four sensors share one outlet. Forward only the selected sensor; changes on the other three must not affect the result.\n\nYou have built a two-way selector. Now choose how to organize four sources. Start with empty internals and basic gates; different structures are valid."

msgid "exploration.selector4.spec"
msgstr "All ports carry one bit. D0–D3 are data. S1 S0 selects the source; S1 is the high bit.\n\nS1 S0    OUT follows\n0   0       D0\n0   1       D1\n1   0       D2\n1   1       D3\n\nOfficial tests cover 4 selections × 16 data patterns = 64 cases. Only behavior matters. You may copy your own subcircuits; no exact gate count is required.\n\n[[multiplexer|Multiplexer (MUX)]]"

msgid "exploration.selector4.hint.1"
msgstr "Try two groups: how would you choose within each group, then between groups? Alternatively, derive one enabling condition per source."

msgid "exploration.selector4.hint.2"
msgstr "The partial board shows one pair: S0 controls D0 / D1. Extend to the other pair, then let S1 select a group."

msgid "exploration.selector4.hint.3"
msgstr "The reference combines three two-way selectors. Decoding then combining, and other equivalent circuits, are also accepted."

msgid "exploration.selector4.summary"
msgstr "You organized selection into a larger system. Try a different structure: what changes between hierarchy and decoding?"

msgid "experience.next.hardware_foundations.selector4"
msgstr "You organized selection into a larger system. Try a different structure: what changes between hierarchy and decoding?"

msgid "exploration.parity.title"
msgstr "One bit went wrong"

msgid "exploration.parity.description"
msgstr "A transmitted bit might flip. An extra check bit helps the receiver detect this kind of error.\n\nD0–D3 plus CHECK make five bits. A valid message contains an even number of ones. Build ERROR: output 0 for an even count, 1 for an odd count."

msgid "exploration.parity.spec"
msgstr "Every port carries one bit. CHECK is a received input; you do not generate it.\n\nExample: data 1010 + CHECK=0 has two ones → ERROR=0.\nFlip one bit: 0010 + 0 has one one → ERROR=1.\nFlip another: 0110 + 0 has two ones → ERROR=0.\n\nTests cover all 32 inputs. Even parity detects any single-bit flip and any odd number of flips. An even number of flips can pass unnoticed. It cannot locate or repair the error.\n\n[[xor_gate|XOR gate]]"

msgid "exploration.parity.hint.1"
msgstr "You only need odd versus even, not the full count. Which familiar gate flips its result when one input flips?"

msgid "exploration.parity.hint.2"
msgstr "The partial board combines the parity of the first two bits. Combine that result with the next bit, eventually including CHECK."

msgid "exploration.parity.hint.3"
msgstr "The reference XORs all five bits in sequence. A balanced XOR tree is equivalent; tests do not inspect positions or internal IDs."

msgid "exploration.parity.summary"
msgstr "You built an error detector. Flip one debug input, then a second, to see why no alarm does not guarantee an error-free message."

msgid "experience.next.hardware_foundations.parity"
msgstr "You built an error detector. Flip one debug input, then a second, to see why no alarm does not guarantee an error-free message."

msgid "exploration.alarm.title"
msgstr "Keep the alarm"

msgid "exploration.alarm.description"
msgstr "A fault may last only an instant. After FAULT returns to 0, ALARM must stay on until the operator asserts CLEAR.\n\nReuse your completed SR latch. Start with empty internals and decide how to remember a fault while making clear reliable."

msgid "exploration.alarm.spec"
msgstr "FAULT, CLEAR and ALARM each carry one bit. Each row is the next input state:\n\nCLEAR=1: ALARM=0, regardless of FAULT.\nCLEAR=0 and FAULT=1: ALARM=1.\nBoth 0: keep the previous ALARM.\n\nCLEAR has priority. The official sequence resets first, then covers hold, repeated clear, simultaneous fault and clear, and faults after clearing. A raw latch can receive conflicting set/reset inputs; design how to honor the public rule.\n\n[[sr_latch|SR latch]]"

msgid "exploration.alarm.hint.1"
msgstr "Consider permission to set separately from clearing. What must happen to the other control path while clear is active?"

msgid "exploration.alarm.hint.2"
msgstr "The partial board allows FAULT to reach SET only when CLEAR is low. RESET still follows CLEAR directly."

msgid "exploration.alarm.hint.3"
msgstr "The reference uses SET = FAULT AND NOT CLEAR, RESET = CLEAR, then reads SRLatch Q. Equivalent stateful circuits are valid too."

